{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYpESHBScSPZfZUZ1Qp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On October 30, OpenAI announced a commitment, and the announcement stops short of a launch. The company says it will honor a common signal in robots.txt that website operators can use to state their preferences about the use of their content for foundational generative AI training. It gives no implementation date beyond aiming to do so \"in the coming months,\" and it promises another update with details at that point.\n\nThat timing affects what an operator can do today. The article describes no new directive to add and no change that takes effect now. What stays in force are the crawler controls OpenAI already offers through robots.txt, which it says will continue to apply and be respected after the new signal arrives. The shared signal is meant to supplement those controls, so an operator's current rules for OpenAI's crawlers keep their meaning.\n\nThe stated benefit is for operators who deal with several providers. Instead of identifying each crawler and maintaining separate rules for every one, they could communicate one preference to all participating providers. The word \"participating\" matters, because OpenAI calls the effort voluntary and urges other AI providers to honor the signal, which indicates that participation is not yet universal. The article also says the signal draws on the IETF's AI Preferences working group, whose discussions are ongoing.\n\nTaken together, the announcement sets a direction and leaves the test for later: whether OpenAI's implementation matches the description, and how many other providers choose to honor the same signal."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":675,"cache_read_input_tokens":4311,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6